@page "/table"

<CatHead Title="Tables">
    One class on the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>. Write the <code>thead</code> and <code>tbody</code>
    in the page.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
        There is no <code>&lt;DataTable&gt;</code> component and there will not be one. Tables are written
        as markup in the page.
    </div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.Basic" Title="Basic" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Headers are uppercased and muted by the class. <code>.col-muted</code> is for secondary
        columns — timestamps, counts — and stops them from wrapping.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.ClickableRows" Title="Clickable rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.tr-clickable</code> adds the pointer, the hover fill and
        <code>user-select:none</code> so a double click does not select the text. Keep a real
        focusable control in the row for keyboard users — a whole row cannot be tabbed to.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.ToolbarCard" Title="With a toolbar, in a card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>The standard list page: filter bar above, table inside a card.</p>
</CatExample>

<div class="cat-note">
        <strong>Wide tables:</strong> put the table in a container with
        <code>overflow-x:auto</code> and add <code>.sedna-scroll</code> to it, so the horizontal
        scrollbar is themed and the page body never scrolls sideways.
    </div>

    <h2>Extensions</h2>
    <p class="lede">
        Each of these is opt-in, because each costs something. A sticky header needs a scroll
        container, zebra stripes fight with row selection, and the stacked layout throws away column
        alignment.
    </p>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.SortableNumericSelected" Title="Sortable, numeric and selected" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The sort arrow is keyed off <code>aria-sort</code>, which is what a screen reader
        announces, so the arrow and the announcement cannot disagree. Put a
        <code>&lt;button class="th-sort"&gt;</code> inside the header cell filling it — a target
        that is only the label text is smaller than it looks. The arrow reserves its 8px even when
        unsorted, so the header does not shift as the reader sorts one column after another.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.col-num</code> goes on the <code>th</code> <em>and</em> the <code>td</code>: right
        aligned and tabular, so digits line up by place value and two numbers can be compared by
        shape. Row selection is <code>aria-selected</code> on the <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>, and the
        leading rule is what survives a colour-blind palette and a greyscale print.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The header buttons here do nothing, and that is the whole design.</strong> The
        library draws the affordance and announces the state; <em>which</em> column is sorted and in
        what order is your query, and reordering rows is what a Blazor page does natively. Set
        <code>aria-sort</code> from that state and the arrow follows.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.StickyHeader" Title="Sticky header" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.table--sticky</code> needs two things from the markup and silently does nothing
        without them: a scroll container around the table with a <code>max-height</code>, because
        the header sticks to the nearest scrolling ancestor and with none it sticks to the page
        where the topbar already is; and <code>border-collapse: separate</code>, which the class
        sets, because a collapsed table's borders belong to the table rather than the cell and its
        header border would scroll away.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.PinnedColumn" Title="Pinned column" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.table--pin-start</code> is the sideways half of <code>.table--sticky</code>: it holds
        the first column still while the rest of a wide table scrolls under it. Use it for the
        column a row is <em>identified</em> by — four columns in, a reader who cannot see the staff
        ID cannot tell whose row they are reading. <code>.table--pin-end</code> does the same at the
        other edge, which is where a row's actions are.
    </p>
    <p>
        It needs a horizontal scroll container — <code>.sedna-scroll-x</code> — and it sets
        <code>border-collapse: separate</code> for itself, because a collapsed table's cells cannot
        stick at all. Everything else is handled: the pinned cell paints on an opaque surface
        rather than letting columns slide under it, it keeps the zebra stripe, the hover fill and
        the selection marker, the corner cell paints over the sticky header rather than under
        the column beneath it, and nothing a scrolling column carries — a segmented control, a
        switch, a menu trigger — paints over the pinned cell on its way past.
    </p>
    <p>
        The edge shadow appears only while a column is actually hidden behind the pinned one — a
        table that fits its container, and one sitting at the start of its scroll, show none. That
        answer comes from <code>.sedna-scroll-x</code>, which is a
        <code>container-type: scroll-state</code> container; a table scrolled inside a wrapper of
        an app's own keeps the shadow painted throughout.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Scroll this table both ways.</strong> Not for
        <code>.table--stack</code>: below 640px the rows become blocks and there is no sideways
        scroll to pin against.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.ColumnWidths" Title="Column widths" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.table</code> leaves column widths to the browser, which is right until the cells in
        a row are not all short values. Then the browser gives an unbounded column the room it asks
        for and squeezes the rest to their padding — a legible table with a
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> in it rendered as a caret and two clipped characters.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.col-control</code> gives a cell holding an input or a select a floor and makes the
        control fill it, so the column and the control agree on an edge.
        <code>.col-prose</code> lets a sentence or a distinguished name give way instead of taking
        the table, and breaks inside a word because an identifier has nowhere else to break.
        <code>.col-fit</code> is the opposite — an icon, a status mark, a row of buttons — taking
        the width of its content and no more.
    </p>
    <p>
        Put each on the <code>th</code> <em>and</em> the <code>td</code>, the way
        <code>.col-num</code> is used. The numbers are knobs:
        <code>--col-control-min</code>, <code>--col-control-max</code>,
        <code>--col-prose-min</code> and <code>--col-prose-max</code>, set on the cell where a
        column needs a different one.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.ExpandableRows" Title="Expandable rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The detail row is a real <code>&lt;tr class="tr-detail"&gt;</code> after its parent, so it
        moves with it when the table is sorted and is read after it. Its cell spans every column.
        The inset rule on the left is the only thing saying it belongs to the row above — a plain
        indent reads as an unrelated row that happens to start further in.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Shown expanded, and there is nothing here to collapse it.</strong> Which row is open
        is your app's state — a <code>bool</code> per row, and <code>aria-expanded</code> on the
        control that toggles it. There is no <code>sednaUi.table</code>: rendering rows is the
        one thing a Blazor app already does better than a script could.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Table.StackedNarrowScreen" Title="Stacked on a narrow screen" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.table--stack</code> turns each row into a card below 640px, taking each cell's label
        from <code>data-label</code>. <strong>Understand the trade first:</strong> it throws away
        column alignment, so comparing one value across rows stops being possible. It is right for
        a list of records read one at a time, and wrong for anything scanned down a column.
    </p>
    <p>
        Narrow this window to see it. A cell with no <code>data-label</code> — an actions cell —
        keeps the full width instead of leaving an empty gutter.
    </p>
</CatExample>
